# Proposed routing and full-spectrum separation argument

This is new root reasoning continuing PROPOSED_LEMMAS.md, before worker exposure. It is unaccepted and needs a complete proof/check. It proposes a way around the interior Jacobi-response problem without assuming all interior minors are nonzero on a constrained calibration family.

Start with the positive-weight calibrated Jacobi core of section 2 of that proposal. Its 2(d-1) positive mode energies lie in small disjoint neighborhoods on the two sides of E_a-E_0. Additional modes are unnecessary for source attachment. Attach a finite routing tree only to the far end of this core. The routing tree has maximum degree three and enough distinct leaves for all positive Bohr gaps; degree-two subdivisions permit an ordinary nearest-neighbor embedding outside the complete old plant, with exactly one old/new edge at the core root. Put a positive onsite frequency nu above all relevant gaps and core energies on every routing site. Initially set every routing edge hopping to zero. Later set them to small positive rho. Each routing leaf couples to one source chain through xi times an exchange. All routing sites count as bus qubits and are retained.

At rho=0 the bus is a Jacobi path plus isolated positive sites. Its many-qubit XY spectrum is the sums of the core's distinct one-particle energies plus k nu. This free-fermion statement applies to the path only; a branched XY bus at rho>0 must NOT be identified with noninteracting fermions. At rho>0 we will instead use continuity of the actual complete finite qubit spectrum.

Fix N and the strong old-plant spectral data first. Choose core energies in the open pole neighborhoods, nu in a fixed high interval, and a common ferromagnetic scale J_F in a fixed positive interval. At rho=0 the full code/Q coincidences are finitely many equalities of the form

    sum_(j in nonempty subset) mu_j + k nu
       + J_F a + (E_b-E_c) + sum_l omega_l z_l = 0,

where a>=0 is a sum of actual source ferromagnetic eigenvalues at scale one and z_l are integer source-excitation differences. Any Q state either excites the bus (some subset or k nonzero) or is nonsymmetric in at least one source (a>0). Thus every relevant equality has a nonzero coefficient in at least one freely varied parameter. The finite union of these hyperplanes cannot fill the open parameter box. The code may have exact internal degeneracies; those are retained, not part of this exclusion. Strict full P/Q separation can be verified by certified finite matrix spectra once a choice is made. This is proposed as a constructive dense search with strict certificates, not an exact-zero oracle.

The cone calibration applies throughout a sufficiently small open box of core energies. Choose all its physical spectral weights strictly positive. Its derivative with respect to one group of d-1 weights is the invertible positive-side difference matrix. At rho=0 the routing sites are decoupled, so they do not enter plant self-energy. For small nonzero rho, the complete bus one-excitation resolvents vary smoothly. A finite quantitative implicit-function argument should therefore adjust that same group of weights, through their Jacobi matrix and the root coupling v_p, to keep every desired diagonal difference exact. All weights remain positive. The root normalization v_p^2=sum weights must be varied consistently; holding it fixed accidentally changes the calibration derivative. This implicit-function step requires an actual constructive bound or a supplied finite certificate, not a bare assertion that generic solutions exist.

The actual full-space Q/P gap at rho=0 is positive. Small positive routing hoppings and the small calibration adjustment preserve it by a complete operator-norm perturbation bound. No branched-bus free-particle assertion is needed. All base coefficients remain in a fixed compact range independent of N; the required positive rho may shrink with N. Source-end response coefficients may then be tiny, and xi must be chosen only after their inverses and the full corrector constants are computed. No favorable runtime power is promised.

At a Bohr frequency omega_l the core-to-routing-leaf Green cofactor has a unique simple path from the core root through the ENTIRE core and then through the routing tree. Its numerator is the product of the nonzero path hoppings times determinants of the off-path routing subtrees. Those subtree matrices have all onsite energies near the high nu and sufficiently small hopping, so their spectra lie above every Bohr frequency. Their determinants do not vanish. The full denominator is also nonzero by the selected complete separation (or its one-excitation consequence). This tree cofactor identity should prove every required root-to-leaf response is nonzero simultaneously. It avoids the unconstrained interior-minor claim in the earlier proposal.

The single-old-port perturbation is xi v_p B X_root. A source perturbation is xi r_l (sigma_+^leaf sigma_-^source + h.c.). From bus vacuum, a source-lowering/old-raising resonant cross transition has only the allowed time ordering 'source creates bus excitation, old annihilates it'; its coefficient is

    -v_p r_l B_ab [(B_bus-omega_l I)^(-1)]_(root,leaf).

There is no automatic factor two. Its Hermitian reverse is the reverse matrix element. The nonsymmetric source component in that first boundary action is killed by the final code projection, since the second action is on old/bus only and the free source Laplacian preserves its symmetry sector. In contrast, the source self-energy retains its nonsymmetric resolvent part and must be canceled by the quadratic code counterterms proposed earlier. All these are full operator statements that still need proof.

Once each cross coefficient is set to magnitude 2|(L_W)_ab|/t_* using a finite real r_l, the coherent source mean supplies the factor one half. Its initial phase, chosen after T=t_*/xi^2, supplies the argument of the desired matrix element and exp[i(E_a-E_b)T]. The coefficient magnitudes, spectral conditions and corrector constants are independent of this phase choice. Summing the source fluctuation bounds gives a candidate constant at most 2 sum_(a>b)|(L_W)_ab|/t_* times 1/sqrt(N), independent of N, bus response and xi after exact source-self-energy cancellation.

The second-order code generator would then be the desired diagonal logarithm plus the resonant collective off-diagonal terms and a listed scalar. The source-only counterterms, all their positive block reserves, the literal old reserve, the bus reserves and xi/xi^2 interaction reserves must appear in the complete generator and phase. Choose N from the fixed fluctuation bound, select the finite bus and full separation, calculate the finite correction/local-load constants, and only then select xi by the section-8 inequalities. This would prove a common added incident cap for this stronger spectral domain, with a supplied coherent state and no polynomial runtime bound.

Remaining review obligations: exact cone and implicit-function hypotheses, actual finite lattice geometry, tree cofactor identity and its denominator convention, full many-body spectrum argument at rho=0 and perturbation at rho>0, quadratic source resolvent coefficients, complete second-order code calculation, normalization/error budget, phase and reserve accounting. Strong spectral addressability has not been derived from the original polynomial Lie certificate. This proposal neither solves that general domain nor supplies the source state, timing, isolation or physical energy identification.
